\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs57\fsmilli28800 \cf2 \cb3 \ul \ulc2 Course Requirements and Expectations
\f1 \cb1 \ulnone \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 Current Circumstances: 
\f2\b0 Learning takes time and effort. With that said, the current social, economic, and political environment is challenging and appears to be causing frustration, anxiety, and turmoil in daily life. Depending on your specific circumstances, taking classes may be difficult. Furthermore, statistical learning, software development in Python or R, and related topics may seem overwhelming to students with little or no background in programming. These challenging times and complex issues require that we work together to create a learning environment that benefits us all. My goal for the course is to break down these complex topics into meaningful applications that prepare students to make better, data-informed decisions. I assume that mutual respect, purpose, and understanding are foundational to our work.\'a0
\f1 \cb1 \

\f0\b \cb3 Psychological or Disability Needs: 
\f2\b0 In addition to any questions or needs beyond what is listed in the rest of this syllabus, if you require any guidance or assistance with counseling, accommodations for disabilities, or other services, resources are available through Student Disabilities Services and Counseling and Psychological Services. If these resources are not meeting your needs related to this course, please let me know so I can determine how to best assist you.
\f1 \cb1 \

\f0\b \cb3 Attendance:\'a0
\f2\b0 This course is taught in person at scheduled times. Attendance is mandatory per program policy. Each semester has 14 course meetings and a make-up class week, if needed. You need to attend class, submit assignments, and participate in weekly activities.\'a0 Unexcused absences may result in a zero grade for the assignment or participation activity.\'a0 Missing assignments automatically receive a zero. For every day late, there is a gradual reduction in score until the assignment or participation activity closes; if not submitted, the assignment receives a zero. I will follow a "flipped" model, where specifically recorded lectures and other materials are available online before they are covered in class periods.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 \cb3 I want to emphasize that reviewing all the material is essential to grasping the overall content of the course, but the specific time and place you choose to do so are entirely\'a0up to you. If you cannot attend a particular session, that is okay, but absences require a valid excuse. Not using the materials at all is a severe problem and may be resolved through individual meetings with your educational advisor or me. This could lead to course failure, depending on the circumstances.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 Participation: 
\f2\b0 The best way to learn to program is to study and write code. When you attend class, I expect you to engage with the lecture, labs, and each other as required to support learning, and ask questions when you need support or clarification. Also, programming often occurs in collaboration, so I encourage you to learn from one another through participation. However, if unexpected events occur at home or elsewhere that interrupt your involvement, please step away for a few minutes. Please mute your sound and turn off your video if you anticipate an extended interruption.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 \cb3 I may occasionally post check-in polls, quizzes, or requests for feedback throughout the semester, which will be distributed via Canvas. These will not be for credit, but they will shape the direction of the course. I hope you take them seriously and help me by sharing your opinions.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 Assignments: 
\f2\b0 This course includes twelve short take-home assignments and four technical projects. The twelve short take-home assignments are individual projects generally completed in class, each allowing you to demonstrate new fundamental programming skills. Each assignment will consist of quantitative questions to be answered with data and code. You may work in your groups, but you must individually submit your answers and source code for how you answered the questions. If you use code from publicly available sources, you must cite and credit those sources, such as ChatGPT and Stack Overflow. I expect that you will write your code, not someone else.\'a0 The four technical projects are individual, and you will submit those assignments accordingly.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 \cb3 Later in the semester, you will work on a final project that must be completed individually. First, you will submit a project proposal in which you choose a dataset to analyze and investigate in depth using programming. At the end of the semester, you'll offer a final project with code and a written case study report. The final project is the last step of the course; there is no final exam.
\f1 \cb1 \

\f2 \cb3 The project proposal and final project will be graded primarily on your written report: your ideas, your presentation of data, and the way you integrate your technical results from your coding. The proposal and final report are evaluated for professional production. Therefore, your grade will reflect how well you accomplished aspects of publishing your work for a broader audience, and may include mechanical issues such as readability and formatting. Projects completed as part of a previous course or that you are working on for another class may not be proposed or submitted.
\f1 \cb1 \

\f2 \cb3 If you need assistance with writing, resources are available at the Weingarten Learning Center, the Marks Family Writing Center, and SP2 Academic & Writing Support. If you require further assistance with writing or technical presentations, please contact me directly and specify the additional help you need.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 Collaboration: 
\f2\b0 Programming for technical projects must be completed individually, and you must be the one writing your code. That said, seeking help from online resources like Codex, Stack Overflow and Kaggle is a regular part of data science, and students are encouraged to discuss problems with one another if it is helpful. 
\f3\i\b When you submit assignments that reuse code found online, you must include links to your sources
\f2\i0\b0 .
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 \cb3 Student submissions that are almost identical in code and content to those of other students may result in penalties. When you work collaboratively on assignments, you should clearly state in your submission which student you worked with and how the work was separated. All students who submit similar code without acknowledging collaborative work may be penalized, regardless of who wrote the code first. Code is generally subject to the same academic integrity policies as other work.
\f1 \cb1 \

\f2 \cb3 Each student must complete programming work as part of the final project; students are not permitted to split project responsibilities between a technical (programming) student and a non-technical (report writing) student.\'a0 With that said, you can work collaboratively to understand the specifics of the problem or question, but you must submit projects individually.\'a0\'a0
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs57\fsmilli28800 \cf2 \cb3 \ul \ulc2 Academic Integrity
\f1 \cb1 \ulnone \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs32 \cf2 \cb3 Students must adhere to the University's Code of Academic Integrity, available at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://catalog.upenn.edu/pennbook/code-of-academic-integrity/"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 https://catalog.upenn.edu/pennbook/code-of-academic-integrity/}}. Care should be taken to avoid academic integrity violations, including plagiarism, fabrication of information, and multiple submissions (see descriptions below).**\'a0\'a0 Students who engage in any of these actions will be referred to the Office of Student Conduct, which investigates and decides on sanctions in cases of academic dishonesty.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Plagiarism: using the ideas, data, or language of another person or source without specific or proper acknowledgment. Example: copying, in part or its entirety, another person's paper, article, or web-based material and submitting it for an assignment; using someone else's ideas without attribution; not using quotation marks where appropriate; etc.
\f1 \cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fabrication: submitting contrived or altered information in any academic exercise.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 \cb3 Examples: making up data or statistics, citing nonexistent articles, contriving sources, etc.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Multiple submissions: submitting, without prior permission, any work submitted to fulfill another academic requirement.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 \cb3 **Students must consult the instructor if they need clarification on whether something violates the Code of Academic Integrity.
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

